00:07:23.068 --> 00:07:24.610
so that's a boring
part of the class,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.670
let's talk about the
motivation for this course

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.430
and the syllabus.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.730
So this is a class
about control.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:39.790
So I assume that all of you are
knowledgeable about the concept

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:40.690
of control.

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:44.210
But essentially, you have a
system, a physical system,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:46.270
but could also be
a financial system.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.190
And you want to
act on the system

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:53.100
to make sure that this system
has some desired response.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.860
For example, you
may want to control

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.080
the stability of
financial market,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.260
or you want to control
the trajectory of a drone.

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:07.680
At an abstract level,
you have a system.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.060
Again, it could be physical, it
could be in a digital sphere.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:16.140
And you have a
sensor that allows

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.800
you to get some information
about the state of the system.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.380
So if this is a
car, for example,

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.940
the sensor might be
a camera sensor that

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:27.420
gets an observation about
the surrounding environment,

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.660
plus maybe some
additional sensors

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:35.140
that allow you to measure,
for example, your speed.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:37.500
And then based on
the measurement

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:40.539
that you get about the
state of your system

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:44.860
and based on some high level
goal referred to as a reference,

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.780
for example, you want your
vehicle to change lane

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:51.500
or going straight
at a given desired

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.540
speed, you use these
two information, pieces

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.420
of information, to
synthesize a control

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:00.700
action through a
function referred

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.980
to as a controller, that is
then exerted on the system,

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.600
and then the system
produces an output.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.820
For example, the control
could be the steering angle,

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.740
and the throttle of a car.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:17.580
And then you have a response,
so the acceleration of the car.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.820
And then the key
idea here is that you

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.120
keep iterating on this process.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.140
So you compute an action,
you observe the response

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.380
of the system, you
measure that response,

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.620
and then you recalculate
your control action.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.340
As a human, obviously,
you do closed loop control

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:41.200
since the time you were born.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.880
Your eyes are your sensors.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.360
You perceive the environment.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.970
You use that information to
decide how to move your joints.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.530
Your high level reference
will be, for example, I

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.050
want to go to that door.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.290
And then I try to
plan my actions

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.610
and execute those
actions accordingly.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:01.190
So that's the standard
setup of a control problem.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:05.510
So if you're taking a class
in a frequency domain control,

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:09.810
so essentially, PID controls,
that's the plot-- the diagram

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.650
you should be familiar with.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:20.930
Another example is if you
want to control your room

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:21.930
temperature.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.010
In that case, your
home is the system.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.910
The room temperature
will be the output.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.090
That's what you measure
through a thermometer.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.430
Your thermostat setting
will be the reference.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:36.610
Then you use that information to
decide on a gas flow rate that

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:40.290
go-- and that signal goes into
the furnace, that then causes

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.410
a response from the home.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.210
The beauty of control
is that actually, you

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:49.495
can be quite judicious in terms
of how you model the system,

00:10:49.495 --> 00:10:51.620
and this model actually
could be learned from data.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:56.760
For example, if this was a class
in computational fluid dynamics,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.320
and then you pose
the problem of how

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:05.028
do you control or set the
temperature of your shower,

00:11:05.028 --> 00:11:07.320
they would say, well, let's
build a thermodynamic model

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:10.520
about how the water
affects the heat that

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:12.140
propagates on your body.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:14.100
In control, we try to
be a bit more pragmatic.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:16.780
So that model could be
something much more simplified.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:19.532
Or we can bypass
the model itself.

00:11:19.532 --> 00:11:21.240
For example, when you
control the shower,

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.200
basically, what is your rule?

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.100
If you move the knob
right, it's warmer.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:31.320
If you move the knob
left, that's colder.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:32.540
And that's your model.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:36.160
So there is a lot of
judicious reasoning

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:37.180
that goes into modeling.

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:40.280
And we'll see some of that too.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:41.980
Now, of course, life is hard.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:45.390
So you're setting up
your control system.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:48.410
But then you had to face
a number of challenges.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:50.670
For example, there might
be disturbances that affect

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:51.990
the evolution of your system.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:54.270
For example, an
aircraft that is flying.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:56.270
And then there is
a wind gust that

00:11:56.270 --> 00:12:00.910
is perturbing your trajectory
from the desired or nominal

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.070
trajectory.

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.650
Or your sensor may
not be perfect,

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.270
so you might have a
measurement noise.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.750
So these are all
challenges that are well

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:12.190
tackled by classical controls
or frequency-based control

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:14.310
systems, PID controllers.

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:16.830
And in general, what are the
control desiderata that you

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:20.350
would state in a basic
control course, for example,

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.990
Engineering 105
here at Stanford?

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.910
Well, you want your
system to be stable.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:28.450
There are a few different
definitions of stability,

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:30.510
like internal stability,
external stability.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:36.550
But in general, by stability,
we mean that the system

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:37.890
doesn't go out of whack.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.770
So basically, the system
output is under control.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:41.610
It stays bounded.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:44.230
It doesn't do something crazy.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:47.665
We have an objective in
general of tracking something.

00:12:47.665 --> 00:12:48.790
We have a reference signal.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.550
For example, the setting
for your thermostat.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:57.470
We want to make sure that
the hum temperature tracks

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:01.350
as close as possible
that reference signal.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.130
We want to reject disturbances.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:04.935
So we are piloting our--

00:13:04.935 --> 00:13:06.310
I mean, we have
an autopilot that

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:08.390
is controlling the aircraft.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:09.370
There is a wind gust.

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:10.828
We want to make
sure that this wind

00:13:10.828 --> 00:13:13.990
gust doesn't perturb the
trajectory of the aircraft

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.030
too much.

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:18.790
And we want the
controller to be robust.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:20.750
Robust means that
the controller should

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:26.852
be able to perform well, even
if your model is inaccurate.

00:13:26.852 --> 00:13:28.810
And of course, your model
is always inaccurate.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:33.350
It's never like a perfect
replica of the physical system.

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:36.350
Or simply, maybe your
model is very accurate,

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:40.110
but your system is
changing through time

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.100
because, for example,
the temperature

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:46.460
changes and the response of
your actuator might change too.

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:49.980
So you want to make sure
that the controller is

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.700
robust to those changes.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:55.740
So you take a class
in classical controls

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:57.780
such as Engineering 105.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:02.700
And actually, you have tools to
address all of these desiderata.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:04.340
So what is missing?

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.300
Well, there are two things
that are missing here.

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:11.020
First, we haven't
discussed at all how much

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.040
we care about the performance.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.340
There are many different
ways to achieve

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:20.083
the objective of stability, but
is there a best way to do so?

00:14:20.083 --> 00:14:21.500
Like, for example,
the way that is

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:27.020
most economical in terms of how
much control effort we spend.

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:31.220
Second of all, in
this plot here, I'm

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:33.520
saying that someone is
giving you a reference,

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:35.813
but where is this
reference coming from?

00:14:35.813 --> 00:14:37.980
I mean, maybe if you want
to control the temperature

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:39.700
of a room, it is obvious.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.210
But if you want to control
the path of an aircraft,

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.030
where is that path coming from?

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:52.110
This is the so-called
element of planning,

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:55.730
where you want to come up in the
first place with that reference

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:56.930
signal.

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:59.010
And third learning.

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:02.170
So everything we have
discussed so far essentially

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:06.910
assumed that a model
of the system is known.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:08.750
But what if that
is not the case?

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:10.690
Or maybe it's the
case, but as I said,

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.390
the system might change
during operations.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:16.970
So how do we adapt
to those changes?

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.670
So these considerations lead
to the core of this class.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.770
How we go beyond classical
control in such a way

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:27.810
that we can broadly address
these key considerations.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.090
What is the best way
to control the system?

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:36.290
How do we do so if we don't have
a perfect model or acquiring

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:38.430
that model is
excessively complicated

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.280
and so we want to
learn it from data.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:43.240
And how we make sure that all
of the components of our closed

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:46.360
loop, also the reference
signal is accounted

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:48.560
for in our control analysis.

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.680
So that's really
the objective of

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:52.500
optimal learning-based control.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:55.160
Optimality speaks to the
performance objective.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:59.800
Learning speaks to the desire
of learning a model from data,

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:04.320
adapting the controller based
on the data that you acquire.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.560
As I said, optimal
learning-based control

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.670
has a long history.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:12.840
It dates back about 50 years.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:16.840
It started actually
during the Cold War.

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:20.080
The motivation was to
optimize the trajectory

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.340
of ballistic missiles both ways.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:25.480
And accordingly, there was a
pioneer in the United States

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:27.520
and a pioneer in
the Soviet Union.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:29.840
The pioneer in the
United States, his name

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:31.560
is Richard Bellman.

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:34.020
If you had taken a class in
decision-making and so on,

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.860
you might have heard of
the Bellman equation.

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:38.560
So it's that Bellman.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.800
And in the Soviet
Union, the pioneer

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:46.320
was Pontryagin, which was a
blind Soviet mathematician.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:48.520
And they developed
their theories

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:50.180
in a fairly independent way.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.400
Of course, there was
not a lot of knowledge

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:53.860
sharing at that time.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:57.420
But interestingly, there
are a lot of commonalities.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:59.940
I mean, the theories and the
methods are a bit different,

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:04.160
but there are a lot of
commonalities between the two.

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:09.160
So this is how our class
is going to be structured.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.960
So first of all,
there is a difference

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:16.640
between the notion of open loop
optimal control and a closed

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.400
loop optimal control.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.040
We'll discuss this a
bit more formally later.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.140
But basically, in open
loop optimal control,

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:26.819
we compute a sequence
of control actions,

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:30.920
and we assume that we're going
to blindly follow those, execute

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:36.566
those control actions without
acquiring further information

00:17:36.566 --> 00:17:37.830
form the word.

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:40.470
Essentially, I want
to go to that door.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.850
I plan my trajectory.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.170
I close my eyes, and
then I hope for the best.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:46.150
So that's open loop control.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:49.050
Of course, it has
its challenges.

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:52.470
So, for example,
if he stands up,

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:56.110
and I have my eyes closed
and I crash into him.

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:58.210
So that motivates
closed loop control.

00:17:58.210 --> 00:17:58.930
Yeah, sure.

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:01.190
You plan your action, but
with the understanding

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:04.050
that you're going to
constantly close the loop.

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:08.270
So measuring and
re-optimize your actions.

00:18:08.270 --> 00:18:14.870
So we're going to first look
at methods where we assume

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:16.930
an open loop control framework.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:17.430
So why?

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:20.170
It seems that open loop is
less powerful than closed loop.

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:21.270
And that's true.

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:24.870
The advantage of open loop is
that it's extremely efficient

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.270
computationally.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:28.430
So it does have an advantage.

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:31.210
So it's useful to start
looking at these methods.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:34.230
And for some
applications actually,

00:18:34.230 --> 00:18:40.060
open loop, as we will discuss,
is a useful control methodology.

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:42.400
Within the open loop
class of methods,

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:45.280
we have two different class
of methods, indirect methods

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:46.320
and direct methods.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.840
I will explain the
differences later on.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.400
But essentially,
the first two weeks,

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:53.820
three weeks of the quarter, we
will focus on open loop control

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:54.940
methodologies.

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:59.380
Roughly speaking, very
efficient computationally,

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:03.440
but potentially not robust
to changes in environment.

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:06.780
Like, for example, your
colleagues standing up.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:11.780
Closed loop, much more powerful,
account for disturbances

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:14.635
natively, but computationally,
more complicated

00:19:14.635 --> 00:19:16.260
because, of course,
you have to account

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:18.540
for different
contingencies that might

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:22.580
occur during the execution
of your control loop.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:26.400
Within the closed loop branch,
there are two subbranches.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:28.052
Dynamic programming, DP.

00:19:28.052 --> 00:19:30.260
Those of you that have taken
class in decision-making

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.460
may already be familiar
with dynamic programming.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:36.060
And the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellmans
and Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:39.380
equations that are basically
continuous time formulation

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:43.340
of dynamic programming,
useful because they provide

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:47.780
the so-called reachability
control paradigms that

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:51.175
are useful to build a safety
guardrails around controllers.

00:19:51.175 --> 00:19:52.800
We'll see all of this
stuff later here.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.900
I want to give you a
high-level overview

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:56.260
of what we're going to do.

00:19:56.260 --> 00:20:00.260
So about three weeks here,
a couple of weeks here.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:03.460
So we said open loop
computationally very efficient,

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:08.700
but potentially brittle as
a optimal control strategy.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:12.580
Closed loop, powerful, but
also computationally expensive.

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:14.540
Is there a best of both worlds?

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:17.660
Yes, that is called MPC,
model predictive control.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:22.020
So we will see-- we will use
MPC, which after PID control,

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:27.380
I will claim is the most
widespread control methodology.

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:30.820
We will frame it as a bridge
between a closed loop control

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:32.580
and open loop control.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:35.930
Roughly what you do, you
solve a problem in open loop,

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:40.910
and then you execute the first
chunk of your control sequence,

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:44.570
and then you close the loop and
recompute your open loop control

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:45.398
sequence.

00:20:45.398 --> 00:20:47.690
So at any point in time, you
solve an open loop control

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:48.510
problem.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:51.330
But the way that the
MPC gets executed

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:53.710
is by iteratively
taking measurements.

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:55.590
I mean, this will
become clearer later.

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:58.430
And of course, there are
variations on the theme,

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.330
but this is at a high-level
what's happening with MPC.

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:05.970
So we spend about two
or three lectures here.

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:08.850
So over here on the
bottom side assumes

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:11.250
that we know a model
of the system, at least

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:13.170
an approximate model.

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:18.770
In the second part of the class,
we will forego that assumption.

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:22.450
And we want to assume anymore
that we have a direct knowledge

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:23.830
of the model of the system.

00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:27.850
So we had to use data in
order to infer that model.

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:33.520
So we'll be using a well-known
ideas from the field of control,

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:37.480
where, for example, the model
has to be identified from data

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:41.480
and use that ideas to set
up the data-driven version

00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:45.400
of optimal control or adaptive
version of the optimal control.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:46.840
So basically,
optimal control when

00:21:46.840 --> 00:21:49.400
the model is not known exactly.

00:21:49.400 --> 00:21:52.560
And we will look at
the three broad methods

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:53.820
to solve the problem.

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:57.300
So the first one is
imitation learning.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:00.680
So roughly speaking, we
just get some demonstration

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:03.280
of what an optimal
policy looks like.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:05.840
And we try to mimic it.

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:08.600
That's a possible way to
compute an optimal policy.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:11.740
And then we're going to look
at reinforcement learning.

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:15.680
So instead of just
mimicking like an expert,

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:18.520
like a teacher
such as myself, you

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:21.920
try to learn by trial and error.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:25.280
And there are a couple of ways
to do so, model based and model

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:26.720
free.

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:32.210
In general, nothing is in
dichotomy with each other.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:35.510
So there are many different ways
to blend all these techniques.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:37.530
In fact, it's quite
common, for example,

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:40.390
to learn a driving policy
in autonomous driving

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:43.630
through imitation learning
to bootstrap a good policy,

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:48.870
and then improve on that policy
through reinforcement learning.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:51.310
That is also true, for
example, for reasoning models

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:52.330
for autonomy.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:55.110
You start first with a
supervised fine-tuning training

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:59.030
pipeline, and then you
try to further improve

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:01.870
your model with different
instantiation of reinforcement

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:02.470
learning.

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:06.230
So I will highlight
different ways

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:09.390
of hybridizing these concept,
but please keep in mind

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.550
that you shouldn't view
each concept in competition

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:13.250
with each other.

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:16.770
There are a lot of ways to blend
all these techniques together.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:21.090
So s half of the course, we
assume that we know the model.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:25.390
We're going to look at two broad
techniques, open loop optimal

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:27.150
control, closed loop control.

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:30.547
And then we look at MPC as
a bridge between the two.

00:23:30.547 --> 00:23:32.130
Then, in the second
half of the class,

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:34.870
we'll move into data-driven
optimal control,

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:37.470
and we'll be looking at the
three main methodologies,

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:39.070
imitation learning
and reinforcement

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:44.910
learning through its model-based
and model-free implementation.

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:48.070
Any questions here?

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:50.110
The beauty of this
class is that you

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:53.730
have a very holistic
overview of all the methods,

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:57.070
and that's important because
in practice, these methods get

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:58.470
hybridized.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:01.630
So it's important
that you understand

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:03.090
model-free
reinforcement learning,

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:07.550
but you do also understand a
more classical direct methods

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:10.550
for optimal control entails.

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:15.470
In modern robotic systems,
we use all of those,

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:18.350
despite the hype that
visual language transfer

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:20.150
models are solving everything.

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:24.605
All right.

00:24:24.605 --> 00:24:26.230
Great.

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:28.740
So what is the course
goal is to learn

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:30.300
the theoretical
and implementation

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:32.960
aspects of main techniques in
optimal learning-based control.

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:34.500
So that's why in
this course, you're

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:37.120
going to have both a
theoretical exercises,

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:42.100
but also practical exercises
mostly through Python.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:46.380
And second, I want to do so by
providing a unified framework.

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:50.940
So not just classical
optimal control

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:54.380
or not just the
decision-making version

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:57.380
of optimal control, which will
be dynamic programming, not

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.780
just reinforcement
learning, but actually

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:01.540
an overarching
theme that connects

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:04.100
all these methodologies,
so that you, as a designer,

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:09.740
know when to use the best tool.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:11.718
Never get in love too
much with a given tool.

00:25:11.718 --> 00:25:13.260
It's very common
for the technologies

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:17.780
to really get in love with some
version of imitation learning

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:20.820
and then use the tool for every
possible problem in the world.

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:24.160
That's not how robotics
or optimal control works.

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:29.130
Any questions?

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.010
Are you excited?

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:33.990
So I think we
mentioned [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.450
And so when we're talking
about optimal [INAUDIBLE],

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:47.018
are we defining
optimality by [INAUDIBLE]?

00:25:47.018 --> 00:25:48.970
one of these metrics?

00:25:48.970 --> 00:25:50.770
Yeah.

00:25:50.770 --> 00:25:55.010
And give me 10 minutes,
and we'll talk a bit more

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:57.590
about the performance index.

00:25:57.590 --> 00:25:58.590
Yes, it's up to you.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:00.390
It's up to the
designer to decide.

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:02.330
But definitely it
can take into account

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:05.590
the desire that your system
doesn't go out of whack,

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:06.450
for sure.

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:07.970
But in addition,
it might also take

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:11.430
into account control effort.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:14.490
So you want to do so, but
minimizing your control effort

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:15.730
as much as possible.

00:26:15.730 --> 00:26:18.210
You might take
into account time.

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:21.250
You want to do something
as quickly as possible.

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:23.250
But by the way,
that is typically

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.968
at odds with the
minimizing control effort.

00:26:25.968 --> 00:26:28.010
Typically, if you want to
do something very fast,

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:30.210
you have to spend more fuel.

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.330
So then there is
a question of how

00:26:32.330 --> 00:26:35.750
do you trade-off potentially
conflicting objectives.

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:39.350
So we'll see in a second how,
for the sake of this class,

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:42.610
but in general, the field of
optimal control, the performance

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:45.410
index is derived.

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:48.450
And it's a fairly
general formulation

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:52.210
that allows you to encode many
of these considerations, which

00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:58.490
is a good segue into how do
we define an optimal control

00:26:58.490 --> 00:26:59.070
problem.

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:00.670
So I've talked at
length about it.

00:27:00.670 --> 00:27:02.050
What is it?

00:27:02.050 --> 00:27:04.810
Interestingly enough,
so all the methodologies

00:27:04.810 --> 00:27:07.010
that I've shown you
before in that [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:08.385
slide-- by the
way, there will be

00:27:08.385 --> 00:27:12.130
the agenda that will stay with
us for the rest of the quarter.

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:18.010
So all those methodologies
solve exactly the same problem.

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:20.370
And the same problem
that I'm going

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:26.080
to show to you in a
second, its formulation

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:29.040
entails three main ingredients.

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:31.160
So mathematical
description of the system

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:34.585
to be controlled that
other we know it,

00:27:34.585 --> 00:27:35.960
or maybe we don't
know it, but we

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:39.640
assume that there
is some underlying

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:45.240
system with mathematical model
that is being controlled.

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:49.320
The second ingredient is
constraints that are typically

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:53.060
not explicitly discussed in
classical feedback control,

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:55.880
but in optimal control,
we want to account

00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:57.940
for them, which is crucial.

00:27:57.940 --> 00:27:59.880
For example, in
autonomous car, you

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:03.720
don't want to crash
on another car.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:05.400
So that's a constraint.

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:09.800
And then as your colleague was
alluding to, the specification

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:12.520
of performance criteria.

00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:15.000
So what we mean by
mathematical model?

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:19.500
Nothing particularly
fancy or new here.

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:22.080
For us, the mathematical
model of a system

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:27.030
them is a set of ordinary
differential equations.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:34.150
So here, x1, x2 xn represent
the state of the system.

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:37.070
So basically, the
set of variables

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:40.910
that, together with the
notion of the control,

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:47.230
allow us to predict what the
system will do in the future.

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:51.470
The u's you want to [INAUDIBLE],
which are the control inputs.

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:53.990
In general, you may have
a multiple control inputs,

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:56.930
throttle command and steering
command in the case of a car.

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:03.550
And then functions
f1, f2 all the way

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:07.030
to fn, that tells us how
the state will evolve

00:29:07.030 --> 00:29:09.670
in the future as a function of
the current value of the state

00:29:09.670 --> 00:29:13.370
variables, the current value
of the control variables.

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:17.270
So for us, the
notion that allows

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:19.150
us to map the
present in the future

00:29:19.150 --> 00:29:21.900
is the notion of an ordinary
differential equation.

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:24.980
Now, if you see an ODE,
it may seem a bit cryptic

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:27.820
why I'm saying that an ordinary
differential equation is

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:31.860
the mechanism we use in
order to reason about how

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:36.300
the future-- how a system
will evolve in the future.

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:38.700
But my pragmatic view
of differential equation

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:40.500
is as follows.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:50.620
So you have x dot t
equal a of xt ut and t.

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:54.940
By the way in this class, a dot
means a derivative with respect

00:29:54.940 --> 00:29:56.540
to time.

00:29:56.540 --> 00:30:00.500
Two dots means a double
derivative with respect to time.

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:04.380
So then saying-- oh, instead
of calling a let me call it f.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:05.940
So this is a
simplified case where

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:10.580
we have one state variable,
one control variable, and then

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:13.140
one function f that tells
us how the state will

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:14.340
evolve in the future.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.620
So why I'm saying that
an ordinary differential

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.940
equation from the perspective
of optimal control

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:22.397
is the mechanisms
that allow us to claim

00:30:22.397 --> 00:30:23.980
that we understand
how the system will

00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:25.700
evolve in the future?

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:32.260
Well, remember that x dot
is equal to, by definition,

00:30:32.260 --> 00:30:36.780
derivative with
respect to time of xt.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:38.940
And the derivative
can be approximated

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:46.620
as xt plus delta t minus
x of t divided by delta t,

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:53.140
where delta t is a small time
increment, say 0.1 seconds.

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:57.900
So if you put this back
in here, what you get--

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.200
oops, let me put it here.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:05.020
So you put this
one back in here.

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:12.780
So what you get is that
xt plus delta t minus xt

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:16.940
over delta t is equal to a of--

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:30.850
f of xt ut t, which implies
that x of t plus delta t

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:42.490
equal xt plus delta t
of f of xt ut and t.

00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:46.490
So you see that if you know
the value of the state x

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:51.650
and the value of the control
u, and you know the function f,

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:54.170
then you also know what the
value of the state will be,

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:58.130
delta t time steps
in the future.

00:31:58.130 --> 00:32:00.650
So that's why when a
mathematician sees an ODE

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:04.490
since a beautiful mathematical
object, when I see an ODE,

00:32:04.490 --> 00:32:08.050
I see a mechanism to
predict the future.

00:32:08.050 --> 00:32:09.370
Does that make sense?

00:32:09.370 --> 00:32:14.290
So that makes basically all
of this mathematical stuff

00:32:14.290 --> 00:32:16.190
a little bit less cryptic.

00:32:19.940 --> 00:32:21.440
And in this class,
in general, we're

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:24.340
assuming that the
f can be anything.

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:26.140
So we're not assuming linearity.

00:32:26.140 --> 00:32:27.840
So we're already
moving quite a bit

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:31.240
away from the more
standard controlled setup

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:34.340
where we make a linear
assumption into your system.

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:41.200
Just for notational
purposes, we typically

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:47.320
define a bold vector
x, so this bold,

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:54.200
as the set of state
variables, x1t, xnt,

00:32:54.200 --> 00:33:00.560
and the set of u variables
ut, which is also bold.

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:04.040
You want tumt.

00:33:04.040 --> 00:33:08.440
And if you do so, we can write
the set of ordinary differential

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:11.580
equations in a more
compact form, as in here,

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:14.000
where now, f is the
concatenation of all

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:17.840
the functions f1, f2,
all the way to fn.

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:19.960
One thing I like to
note here is that

00:33:19.960 --> 00:33:23.980
at some time, this mechanism
to predict the future,

00:33:23.980 --> 00:33:27.320
so what the next state
would be, might not only

00:33:27.320 --> 00:33:31.400
be a function of time
or state and control,

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:34.880
but also an explicit
function of time.

00:33:34.880 --> 00:33:37.240
Which means that in
some systems, given

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:39.280
the same state and
control, depending

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:42.680
on when you hit the
specific value of the state,

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:44.680
and for the same
value control, you

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:49.080
might get a different
response depending on time.

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:53.680
For example, maybe it
could be day or night.

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:56.080
Your system might respond
quite differently,

00:33:56.080 --> 00:33:59.040
whether you are during the
day or during the night even

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:01.720
for the same state and control.

00:34:01.720 --> 00:34:03.640
A history of
control input values

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:05.058
is called the control history.

00:34:05.058 --> 00:34:07.600
This is just terminology, but
we'll be using this terminology

00:34:07.600 --> 00:34:08.560
quite a bit.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:12.040
A history of state values
during interval t0 to tf

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.030
is called a trajectory.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:17.735
T0 is typically referred to as
the initial time of operations.

00:34:17.735 --> 00:34:19.110
That's the symbol
we'll be using.

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:26.310
Tf stands for final, is the
final time of operations.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:28.989
Let me give you a very simple
example that you should already

00:34:28.989 --> 00:34:31.409
be familiar with, a
double integrator.

00:34:31.409 --> 00:34:36.590
So we have a cart moving
on the horizontal plane.

00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:39.310
We only have one
state variable, which

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:41.670
is the displacement
of the center

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:43.650
of the cart from the origin.

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:45.870
So we assume that
the origin is here.

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:49.830
So s is telling us how
much we travel on the left

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:53.190
or to the right of the origin.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:56.550
So we assume that we
are just controlling

00:34:56.550 --> 00:35:01.030
the acceleration of this cart.

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:04.590
So the equations of motion
is that the acceleration

00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:05.410
of the system.

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:09.410
So the double derivative is
equal to the input we apply.

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:12.090
So essentially, the
force we apply to it.

00:35:15.740 --> 00:35:18.820
Well, basically, we're assuming
that here the mass of this cart

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:20.620
is equal to 1.

00:35:20.620 --> 00:35:22.795
So this is our dynamical model.

00:35:22.795 --> 00:35:24.420
The only point that
I want to make here

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:27.780
is that sometimes
dynamical models come

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:32.220
in the form of a second
order differential equations,

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:35.520
or dynamical systems should
be second order systems,

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:39.900
since you evolve
at a basic level,

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:42.020
at the level of accelerations.

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:44.800
So now, you might say, well,
this is a second order system,

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:46.740
but you're saying
that in this class,

00:35:46.740 --> 00:35:50.740
we focus on models where we
have a system of first order

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:52.860
differential equations.

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:55.140
Can we reconcile the two?

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:56.240
And the answer is yes.

00:35:56.240 --> 00:35:58.820
As you may already
know, you can always

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:01.860
map a higher order
differential equation

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:04.680
into a system of first
order differential equation.

00:36:04.680 --> 00:36:06.580
And this is a
one-to-one mapping.

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:10.420
So for example, here,
how does that work?

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:16.370
It works by adding one state
variable, which in this case

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:20.750
would be denoted by v and
encodes the notion of speed.

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:29.430
So we reason in terms
of two state variables.

00:36:29.430 --> 00:36:34.930
So we augment the state vector
with a new state variable v.

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:41.850
And then s dot is equal to v.
And this is, by definition,

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:45.690
the time derivative
of the displacement

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:47.450
is equal to velocity.

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:49.370
And then the time
derivative of the velocity

00:36:49.370 --> 00:36:51.310
is equal to the acceleration.

00:36:51.310 --> 00:37:00.210
That is what our
input represents.

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:05.330
So you see how we went from
a second order differential

00:37:05.330 --> 00:37:08.850
equation describing our system
into a set of two first order

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:10.070
differential equation.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.513
And we are back to
our basic setup.

00:37:12.513 --> 00:37:13.930
So bottom line is
that if you have

00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:17.410
a system that is described by
a higher order differential

00:37:17.410 --> 00:37:20.010
equations, before you apply
the techniques explained

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:22.450
in this class, first,
you translate it

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:24.790
into a set of first order
differential equations.

00:37:24.790 --> 00:37:28.030
And then we're back to
the standard formalism.

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:31.370
And that can always
be done by using

00:37:31.370 --> 00:37:33.310
the trick of adding states.

00:37:36.290 --> 00:37:38.650
This case specifically
is an interesting one,

00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:41.930
because this is a linear system.

00:37:41.930 --> 00:37:43.710
So if you're familiar
with linear systems,

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:46.890
typically you present
them in matrix format

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:51.490
where you have a state
matrix and an input matrix.

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:54.770
Not important now,
but just to link this

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:58.210
back to what you may already
know from a classical control

00:37:58.210 --> 00:37:58.830
theory.

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:02.370
So you can write
the system in terms

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:05.050
of a matrix A times
the state vector

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:08.530
plus a matrix B times
the input vector.

00:38:08.530 --> 00:38:11.700
In this case, the input vector
is just one control variable,

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:15.260
so it's just A. A is referred
to as the state matrix.

00:38:15.260 --> 00:38:17.420
B is referred to as
the input matrix.

00:38:20.360 --> 00:38:23.080
OK, that's what I wanted
to say about models.

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:25.600
Constraints.

00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:28.500
Clearly, in general, when
you control a system,

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:31.820
you have to reason
about constraints.

00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:35.640
For a physical system, we
don't want to hit a wall.

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:39.480
Of course, the amount of control
we have available is bounded.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:42.320
We cannot have a trust level
that is above a certain

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:42.940
quantity.

00:38:42.940 --> 00:38:45.800
Sometimes it can be
below a certain quantity.

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:48.240
So there are, accordingly,
different ways

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:50.040
of setting constraints.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:53.560
Typically, we have a constraint
on our initial condition.

00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:56.280
So we know where we start from.

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:58.120
Sometimes we might
have a constraint

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:00.100
on our desired final condition.

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:03.560
We want to hit a
very precise point.

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:08.550
Like, for example, you want
to send a spacecraft to Mars.

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:10.870
And you want to land
on the Gale crater.

00:39:10.870 --> 00:39:12.270
I mean, just to make it up.

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:15.010
Before joining Stanford,
I was an employee of JPL.

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:17.230
So that's the problem
that I was looking at.

00:39:17.230 --> 00:39:19.910
So landing on a
specific point on Mars

00:39:19.910 --> 00:39:25.750
is your terminal constraint.

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:26.730
Sometimes you have it.

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:28.350
Sometimes you don't have it.

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:31.270
Typically, you want your state
to avoid some bad regions

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:33.910
of the state space,
which could be formulated

00:39:33.910 --> 00:39:37.130
as a lower bound and upper
bound for the state variables.

00:39:37.130 --> 00:39:40.030
But there are also
more creative ways

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:41.450
of setting up state constraints.

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:43.772
For example, you want to
stay outside of a circle.

00:39:43.772 --> 00:39:45.230
A circle could be
a person, and you

00:39:45.230 --> 00:39:50.070
want your robot to stay outside
of the physical envelope

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:51.310
of that person.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:53.270
You want your control
input to satisfy

00:39:53.270 --> 00:39:56.190
lower bound input and
lower bound and upper bound

00:39:56.190 --> 00:39:59.230
constraints and so on.

00:39:59.230 --> 00:40:03.782
So that's pretty intuitive.

00:40:03.782 --> 00:40:05.990
There is a lot of freedom
in optimal control in terms

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:10.000
of how you state your
constraints, with some caveats.

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:12.500
Of course, if you have a
very convoluted expressions

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:14.780
of your constraints, then
the mathematical procedures

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:16.580
we're going to
see later on might

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:18.980
get a bit more complicated.

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:22.340
A control history that satisfies
the control constraints

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:25.100
are referred to as
an admissible control

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:27.488
if they satisfy the
constraints at all time.

00:40:27.488 --> 00:40:29.780
And the state trajectory that
satisfies the constraints

00:40:29.780 --> 00:40:34.060
at all times is referred to
as an admissible trajectory.

00:40:36.955 --> 00:40:38.780
Three, performance measure.

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:40.460
Optimal control
stands for the fact

00:40:40.460 --> 00:40:43.020
that we are trying to find a
control sequence or control

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:48.140
policy that is the best possible
one you can come up with.

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:49.580
The best with respect to what?

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:52.780
I mean, that's the role of
the performance measure.

00:40:52.780 --> 00:40:55.620
There are different ways you can
set up a performance measure.

00:40:55.620 --> 00:41:00.300
In this class, we look only
at this performance measure

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:03.580
here, which might
sound a bit cryptic.

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:07.060
So my point now is I'm going to
spend three minutes to show you

00:41:07.060 --> 00:41:09.000
that this is not cryptic at all.

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:10.580
It's actually very intuitive.

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:14.820
It's not the most general way of
defining a performance measure,

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:19.660
but it provides a good
trade-off between what

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:23.380
at the end of the day,
is computationally

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:27.140
feasible in terms of
synthesizing the control law

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:29.060
and also being general.

00:41:29.060 --> 00:41:32.620
That's always a little bit
tension in optimal control.

00:41:32.620 --> 00:41:35.260
You want to be as
sophisticated as you can

00:41:35.260 --> 00:41:37.428
in how you express the
problem, but to the point

00:41:37.428 --> 00:41:38.720
that you can solve the problem.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:42.300
If you make it too
complicated, if you

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:45.180
can't solve it in a
reasonable amount of time,

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:47.100
then it's useless.

00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:52.300
OK, so what does this
performance measure really mean?

00:41:52.300 --> 00:41:59.020
So this performance measure
has two main components,

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:01.860
this component here
with the H function

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:05.170
and this component
here with the integral.

00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:15.690
So first, let's start with
the h of x tf, h of x tf, tf.

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:23.590
So this is referred to
as the terminal cost.

00:42:28.302 --> 00:42:30.745
Tf is the final time.

00:42:30.745 --> 00:42:32.370
As a reminder, we
assume that we solved

00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:37.210
the problem between time
t0 to the final time tf,

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:40.170
say 10 seconds from now.

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:43.290
And so this measures
how unhappy we

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:46.190
are if after applying
the control sequence

00:42:46.190 --> 00:42:54.170
we have decided we end up
in state x tf at time tf.

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:58.450
For example, say that
we want to do tracking.

00:42:58.450 --> 00:43:04.280
Ideally, we want to arrive
at a very specific location

00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:05.840
at a final time.

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:10.160
In which case, the
function hx tf,

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:15.580
tf could take the form of x
evaluated at the final time,

00:43:15.580 --> 00:43:19.760
so x tf minus f, which
is your desired position

00:43:19.760 --> 00:43:22.480
at the final time squared.

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:27.920
So that if your state under
your control hits the state

00:43:27.920 --> 00:43:32.320
x tf that is equal xf, that is
what you want, then this is 0.

00:43:32.320 --> 00:43:34.060
But the moment there
is a deviation,

00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:36.080
this cost starts growing.

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:39.280
So this is an example of how
you might use the terminal

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:44.480
cost to promote a
control strategy that

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:48.060
brings you as close as possible
at your desired destination,

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:51.440
except at the final time.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:52.500
Any questions here?

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:02.592
That's the most intuitive part.

00:44:02.592 --> 00:44:04.300
This part is a little
bit less intuitive.

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:05.402
You have an integral.

00:44:05.402 --> 00:44:06.860
It's a bit unclear
what that means.

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:12.040
But basically what this
means, view g as the cost

00:44:12.040 --> 00:44:15.360
that you incur per
unit of time, and then

00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:20.240
view the integral as essentially
a summation from time t0

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:22.800
when you start
operations to time tf.

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:26.320
So basically, these
models are cumulative cost

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:28.840
along your state trajectory.

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:34.780
G of x, u is the state that
you incur at a specific time,

00:44:34.780 --> 00:44:38.440
h is the rate at which
you incur that cost

00:44:38.440 --> 00:44:41.400
if you're in state x of t
and you use control u of t.

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:44.400
Of course, you don't
care about just the cost

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:46.660
that you incur at time 0.1.

00:44:46.660 --> 00:44:49.800
You care about the
cumulative cost in general.

00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:53.880
So that's why you
take an integral.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:59.360
So this first term here
models how unhappy you

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:01.550
are at the end of operations.

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:06.270
This term here is a
cumulative cost term

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:08.270
that puts together
all the costs that

00:45:08.270 --> 00:45:11.830
are incurred throughout the
execution of your control

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:13.110
trajectory.

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:16.390
So for example, typical--

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:27.050
so g is referred to as
the stage-wise cost.

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:30.230
It's basically the cost that
you accrue per unit of time.

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:36.350
Typical cost could
be ut squared.

00:45:36.350 --> 00:45:40.430
So these models control
effort use square of n times

00:45:40.430 --> 00:45:41.950
as a notion of energy.

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:45.330
So that's how much energy you
are spending per unit of time.

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:46.490
You take an integral.

00:45:46.490 --> 00:45:50.190
You have the overall
fuel that you have spent.

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:52.330
Of course, many other
formulations are possible.

00:45:55.670 --> 00:45:57.770
If you're familiar with
dynamic programming,

00:45:57.770 --> 00:46:00.700
that's not too different from
your dynamic programming cost.

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:03.220
Well, simply because you're
solving the same problem.

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:05.480
So this becomes a
submission, if you remember,

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:06.980
from dynamic programming.

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:12.340
So your cost is basically a
summation of stage-wise cost.

00:46:12.340 --> 00:46:14.443
So the framework is
exactly the same.

00:46:14.443 --> 00:46:16.693
Are you referring to stage-wise,
or your running cost,

00:46:16.693 --> 00:46:17.340
as like your--

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:18.520
to compute in it?

00:46:18.520 --> 00:46:25.420
Or, let's say, you make an error
[INAUDIBLE] around this way

00:46:25.420 --> 00:46:29.780
or do that way once [INAUDIBLE]?

00:46:29.780 --> 00:46:31.820
Is that all for--

00:46:31.820 --> 00:46:32.880
All labs there.

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:45.660
So for example, your
g could be x of t

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:51.780
minus x reference
at time t squared.

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:55.320
So this is your desired
trajectory that you want to hit.

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:57.490
And this is your
actual trajectory.

00:46:57.490 --> 00:47:00.630
And then your x tf,
tf, for example,

00:47:00.630 --> 00:47:02.810
could be equal to tf squared.

00:47:02.810 --> 00:47:08.330
You are also penalizing how
long it took to get to a given.

00:47:08.330 --> 00:47:10.150
Which brings to an
interesting point.

00:47:10.150 --> 00:47:13.890
So t0, in general, is given,
is the initial time operations.

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:18.272
Tf might be given or might be
subject to optimization too.

00:47:18.272 --> 00:47:19.730
So in some cases,
you know that you

00:47:19.730 --> 00:47:23.170
want to optimize your
operations within a time

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:24.270
window of 10 seconds.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:26.170
In which case, tf
is equal to 10.

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:27.930
But in some other
cases, as you're saying,

00:47:27.930 --> 00:47:33.330
you want to track the trajectory
and get to some point,

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:34.990
but your not specifying
the final time.

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:37.590
You're saying, I just want
to do it as fast as possible,

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:40.990
but at the same time, I want to
keep the trajectory error small.

00:47:40.990 --> 00:47:42.630
So in that case,
tf will be free.

00:47:42.630 --> 00:47:44.890
And so you will
need also to account

00:47:44.890 --> 00:47:49.450
for some cost for the final
time so that you encode

00:47:49.450 --> 00:47:52.130
the notion of minimizing time.

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:55.250
So great question.

00:47:55.250 --> 00:47:58.610
So all of this to say that what
is the optimal control problem

00:47:58.610 --> 00:48:02.030
is the problem of finding an
admissible control u star.

00:48:02.030 --> 00:48:04.490
Admissible means that we
are satisfying the control

00:48:04.490 --> 00:48:08.050
constraints, which
causes the system x

00:48:08.050 --> 00:48:13.650
dot equal f of x ut to follow
an admissible trajectory x star,

00:48:13.650 --> 00:48:17.410
such that u star
and x star jointly

00:48:17.410 --> 00:48:21.450
minimizes this cost function.

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:24.970
Note that once you select
u, x is uniquely determined

00:48:24.970 --> 00:48:26.130
by the ODE.

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:30.050
But of course, the final cost
depends on both the value

00:48:30.050 --> 00:48:32.330
of x and the value of u.

00:48:32.330 --> 00:48:38.130
Your optimization
variable is ut.

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:39.890
As we will see, we can--

00:48:39.890 --> 00:48:41.530
I'll get to your question.

00:48:41.530 --> 00:48:44.190
As we will see, this is
an optimization problem.

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:48.690
We are optimizing over the
space of control sequences ut.

00:48:48.690 --> 00:48:51.390
So it has a strong connection
to the field of optimization,

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:52.890
but there is a catch.

00:48:52.890 --> 00:48:54.600
In optimization,
if you are you're

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:57.400
familiar with
optimization, you typically

00:48:57.400 --> 00:49:04.120
have a finite number
of decision variables,

00:49:04.120 --> 00:49:13.320
minimizing over x1 and x2,
x1 squared plus x2 squared.

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:17.367
So you pick two numbers
that minimizes this thing.

00:49:17.367 --> 00:49:18.700
You have two decision variables.

00:49:18.700 --> 00:49:20.492
You have to decide what
is the value of x1,

00:49:20.492 --> 00:49:22.480
what is the value of x2.

00:49:22.480 --> 00:49:26.800
Here, you are in a much
more challenging situation.

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:29.740
Ut is a function of time.

00:49:29.740 --> 00:49:33.920
So essentially, you want
to pick the optimal values

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:43.880
for ut0, ut0 plus epsilon,
ut0 plus 2 epsilon,

00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:46.940
and so on with epsilon
arbitrarily small.

00:49:46.940 --> 00:49:49.920
So indeed, you don't
have a very large number

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:51.060
of decision variables.

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:53.190
At least in the continuous
time formulation,

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:56.310
you have an infinite
amount of variables.

00:49:56.310 --> 00:49:58.810
So through this class,
you expand your mindset.

00:49:58.810 --> 00:50:03.670
You go from finite dimensions
to infinite dimensions.

00:50:03.670 --> 00:50:05.110
So that's why this
problem is also

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:07.590
referred to as infinite
dimensional optimization

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:09.263
problem.

00:50:09.263 --> 00:50:11.430
So that sounds scary, but
we'll see that actually we

00:50:11.430 --> 00:50:13.150
have tools at our disposal.

00:50:13.150 --> 00:50:16.430
Many of those actually
recycled from the more

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:20.390
classical finite dimensional
optimization literature.

00:50:20.390 --> 00:50:23.590
And of course, I will
highlight these bridges

00:50:23.590 --> 00:50:25.490
in the next couple of lectures.

00:50:28.590 --> 00:50:32.230
Now, you might say, you're
giving us this very specific

00:50:32.230 --> 00:50:33.610
performance criterion.

00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:34.770
Well, a couple of comments.

00:50:34.770 --> 00:50:36.270
First of all, this
is quite general.

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:39.790
There are a lot of
desiderata we can fold

00:50:39.790 --> 00:50:42.590
into this general formulation.

00:50:42.590 --> 00:50:45.350
But second of all, if
you go even more general,

00:50:45.350 --> 00:50:49.070
you could, but then it will
be very, very difficult

00:50:49.070 --> 00:50:51.990
to find solving algorithms.

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:52.950
Why?

00:50:52.950 --> 00:50:55.390
The integral is
essentially a summation.

00:50:55.390 --> 00:50:57.650
So roughly speaking-- I
know that I'm recorded.

00:50:57.650 --> 00:50:59.170
So I hope that you
don't quote me.

00:50:59.170 --> 00:51:02.650
But roughly speaking, what
does the summation gives you?

00:51:02.650 --> 00:51:06.950
Essentially, it allows you to
chop the problem into pieces

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:09.910
and find smart ways
to chopping it.

00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:11.870
For example, this
idea of chopping

00:51:11.870 --> 00:51:15.570
leads in the case of a
discrete time formulation,

00:51:15.570 --> 00:51:18.430
but there's also an analog for
the continuous time formulation

00:51:18.430 --> 00:51:20.830
to the principle optimality.

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:24.550
You might have heard of it if
you study decision-making, which

00:51:24.550 --> 00:51:27.790
allows to dramatically decrease
the number of solutions

00:51:27.790 --> 00:51:29.710
that you have to search over.

00:51:29.710 --> 00:51:33.190
The moment you break this
kind of additive assumption--

00:51:33.190 --> 00:51:36.390
additive structure in your
performance criterion,

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:39.270
all this structure
goes out of the window.

00:51:39.270 --> 00:51:41.990
And so you can't
really use it anymore.

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:45.310
Computation becomes
extremely challenging.

00:51:45.310 --> 00:51:47.030
We see that a bit
better when we talk

00:51:47.030 --> 00:51:50.900
about principle of optimality
and dynamic programming captions

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:51.660
and so on.

00:51:51.660 --> 00:51:56.440
But for now, trust me that
this is a general formulation.

00:51:56.440 --> 00:51:57.873
There is certainly
some structure.

00:51:57.873 --> 00:51:59.540
And the reason why
we put some structure

00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:03.300
is to make sure that actually
we can solve these problems.

00:52:03.300 --> 00:52:07.380
Well, I'm just wondering if
there was any specific reason we

00:52:07.380 --> 00:52:10.380
chose to write it separately?

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:12.480
Because of the integral
of this cumulative,

00:52:12.480 --> 00:52:15.340
it only includes the
final state [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:15.340 --> 00:52:16.320
Yeah, you're right.

00:52:16.320 --> 00:52:18.140
In some cases,
actually, the cost

00:52:18.140 --> 00:52:22.287
can be represented both
in the final and here.

00:52:22.287 --> 00:52:24.120
It depends also a little
bit on convenience.

00:52:27.500 --> 00:52:29.220
So a few comments.

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:33.740
Here, at least in
this formulation,

00:52:33.740 --> 00:52:37.860
we're trying to find
a global minimum.

00:52:37.860 --> 00:52:42.420
Even though sometimes we have to
be happy with a local minimum.

00:52:42.420 --> 00:52:43.440
We do have challenges.

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:44.940
So in addition to
the fact that this

00:52:44.940 --> 00:52:47.500
is an infinite
dimensional problem,

00:52:47.500 --> 00:52:52.225
it is not guaranteed that
actually a solution exists.

00:52:52.225 --> 00:52:54.350
And if it exists, it's not
guaranteed to be unique.

00:52:54.350 --> 00:52:56.017
So this poses some
challenges that we'll

00:52:56.017 --> 00:53:00.410
see in terms of how we
solve these problems.

00:53:00.410 --> 00:53:02.850
Another point is that even
though we solve this problem

00:53:02.850 --> 00:53:07.110
as a minimization, if you
have a maximization problem,

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:10.490
you can always convert
it to this formalism,

00:53:10.490 --> 00:53:13.010
because every maximization
problem can be written

00:53:13.010 --> 00:53:15.650
as a minimization
problem with a minus

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:18.370
sign in front of the objective.

00:53:18.370 --> 00:53:20.638
Interestingly, that
doesn't make sense.

00:53:20.638 --> 00:53:22.430
So if you don't want
to maximize something,

00:53:22.430 --> 00:53:25.090
it's equivalent to
minimizing the minus thing

00:53:25.090 --> 00:53:28.010
that you want to maximize.

00:53:28.010 --> 00:53:31.070
And here, you start seeing
a little bit of a shift.

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:35.970
So the control theoretical
community and the computer

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:38.530
science communities have
developed slightly different

00:53:38.530 --> 00:53:40.890
terminologies.

00:53:40.890 --> 00:53:46.250
So for example, in control, we
refer to the performance index

00:53:46.250 --> 00:53:48.480
as j and we call it cost.

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:51.240
In computer science, it's
referred to as the reward.

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:52.320
It's referred to r.

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:54.920
And the goal is to maximize.

00:53:54.920 --> 00:53:57.200
I'll leave it to you the
psychological considerations

00:53:57.200 --> 00:53:59.900
about why community looks
at maximizing thing,

00:53:59.900 --> 00:54:01.660
and another one
minimizing things.

00:54:01.660 --> 00:54:03.600
But my point is the same.

00:54:03.600 --> 00:54:11.120
Or for example, in
terms of notation,

00:54:11.120 --> 00:54:14.520
in control, we refer
to the state as x.

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:18.580
In computer science,
it's referred to as s.

00:54:21.840 --> 00:54:24.440
In control, we refer
to control as u.

00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:29.200
In computer science,
referred to as a, action.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:32.318
Yeah, I'm iterating
through all of them.

00:54:32.318 --> 00:54:33.860
And this is the
start of the quarter.

00:54:33.860 --> 00:54:35.720
So who knows what
happens at the end?

00:54:40.440 --> 00:54:45.480
Anyways, we'll mostly
be using this notation,

00:54:45.480 --> 00:54:48.080
especially for the first
two-thirds of the class,

00:54:48.080 --> 00:54:52.360
but for the last third with
which to this notation,

00:54:52.360 --> 00:54:57.480
just to be a little bit
more similar to papers

00:54:57.480 --> 00:54:59.047
and publications.

00:54:59.047 --> 00:55:01.380
But I just want to make sure
that you don't get tripped.

00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:06.132
I mean, we're talking about
the same things, x, s, u, a,

00:55:06.132 --> 00:55:07.300
r minus j.

00:55:11.640 --> 00:55:15.920
So a couple of more
things before we

00:55:15.920 --> 00:55:20.200
switch to the first real
topic of this course.

00:55:20.200 --> 00:55:21.500
Forms of optimal control.

00:55:21.500 --> 00:55:24.000
This is something that I
already have alluded to.

00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:25.380
And here, we have to be careful.

00:55:25.380 --> 00:55:29.440
This seems to be a fairly
nuanced difference,

00:55:29.440 --> 00:55:31.380
but extremely important.

00:55:31.380 --> 00:55:33.607
And people get
tripped over and over.

00:55:33.607 --> 00:55:35.440
So I mean, there are
even senior researchers

00:55:35.440 --> 00:55:38.040
that sometimes do not
really properly appreciate

00:55:38.040 --> 00:55:43.400
the difference between open loop
control and closed loop control.

00:55:43.400 --> 00:55:48.750
So as I mentioned, you can
do control in two ways.

00:55:48.750 --> 00:55:54.830
You can either think
of optimizing u purely

00:55:54.830 --> 00:55:58.830
as a function of time
and initial condition,

00:55:58.830 --> 00:56:04.130
or u as a function
of the current state

00:56:04.130 --> 00:56:06.310
and the initial condition.

00:56:06.310 --> 00:56:08.710
As explained, it's
almost impossible

00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:10.430
to understand what I'm saying.

00:56:10.430 --> 00:56:13.770
But again, let me do an example.

00:56:13.770 --> 00:56:15.070
So the same example as before.

00:56:15.070 --> 00:56:17.270
I want to go to that door.

00:56:17.270 --> 00:56:22.110
Optimizing a function of
only time is equivalent to--

00:56:22.110 --> 00:56:26.690
this time here, I optimize
a sequence of steps.

00:56:26.690 --> 00:56:31.750
So basically, the sequence
of steps for my legs.

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:34.750
And then I close my
eyes, so I do not

00:56:34.750 --> 00:56:39.030
allow myself to measure
anything about the current state

00:56:39.030 --> 00:56:40.390
of the world.

00:56:40.390 --> 00:56:45.660
And at time t, I'm going to
use the pre-computed action u

00:56:45.660 --> 00:56:50.900
star of xt0t no matter
what happens around me.

00:56:50.900 --> 00:56:53.260
So if you colleague
stands up, I'm

00:56:53.260 --> 00:56:55.940
going to just crash into him.

00:56:55.940 --> 00:56:59.300
So that's open loop
control, open because you

00:56:59.300 --> 00:57:03.580
don't have this notion of
loop closed or [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:03.580 --> 00:57:06.700
Closed loop control,
you try to optimize over

00:57:06.700 --> 00:57:12.060
u over a broader class
of possible trajectories.

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:15.100
Of course, this is
a subset of this.

00:57:15.100 --> 00:57:19.300
Not only those that map a
time into a given control,

00:57:19.300 --> 00:57:23.140
but also take into account
any possible states

00:57:23.140 --> 00:57:25.740
that you might visit.

00:57:25.740 --> 00:57:27.660
So this is a much
more powerful way

00:57:27.660 --> 00:57:32.260
of parameterizing a
control trajectory,

00:57:32.260 --> 00:57:35.940
because it's telling you not
only what to do if anything goes

00:57:35.940 --> 00:57:38.980
nominal, so you don't
stand up, but also what

00:57:38.980 --> 00:57:41.600
I should be doing if
something goes off nominal,

00:57:41.600 --> 00:57:43.580
so you stand up.

00:57:43.580 --> 00:57:44.520
Of course, to do so.

00:57:44.520 --> 00:57:50.220
I had to keep my eyes open in
order to measure the state.

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:53.200
Pi is referred to
also as policy.

00:57:53.200 --> 00:57:58.220
The policy is the notion of a
function that maps the state

00:57:58.220 --> 00:58:00.780
into an optimal control action.

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:02.460
The question is,
here, communication

00:58:02.460 --> 00:58:06.300
that is [INAUDIBLE] ultimately,
or is that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:58:06.300 --> 00:58:08.820
Pi super common.

00:58:08.820 --> 00:58:10.320
E is somewhat arbitrary.

00:58:10.320 --> 00:58:12.820
Different people have a
slightly different notation.

00:58:12.820 --> 00:58:14.580
I would say, pi is
the gold standard

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:16.100
for the closed loop policy.

00:58:16.100 --> 00:58:20.900
For open loop, no,
also, because typically,

00:58:20.900 --> 00:58:24.180
the initial condition--

00:58:24.180 --> 00:58:26.688
the dependence on initial
condition is just assumed.

00:58:26.688 --> 00:58:27.980
So it's not even made explicit.

00:58:27.980 --> 00:58:30.020
Here, I'm just doing
it to be pedantic.

00:58:30.020 --> 00:58:36.500
And so people just
write u star of t period

00:58:36.500 --> 00:58:40.060
to denote an open loop
control trajectory, and then

00:58:40.060 --> 00:58:44.173
pi of xt and t to
denote a closed loop.

00:58:44.173 --> 00:58:45.590
So this will be
the more standard.

00:58:45.590 --> 00:58:46.923
But here, I want to be pedantic.

00:58:46.923 --> 00:58:49.290
And so I came up with
this additional letter

00:58:49.290 --> 00:58:52.930
e for lack of a better term.

00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:55.110
It seems a bit nuanced,
it seems a bit academic,

00:58:55.110 --> 00:58:56.510
but this is very consequential.

00:58:56.510 --> 00:59:01.250
So I will see over and over
throughout this class methods

00:59:01.250 --> 00:59:04.970
that aim at computing open
loop control sequences, methods

00:59:04.970 --> 00:59:08.770
that are aimed at computing the
more general concept of a closed

00:59:08.770 --> 00:59:11.930
loop policy, and what are the
trade-off thereof in terms

00:59:11.930 --> 00:59:18.170
of computational efficiency
and robustness and so on.

00:59:18.170 --> 00:59:19.370
One more thing.

00:59:19.370 --> 00:59:22.530
I presented a formulation
in continuous time,

00:59:22.530 --> 00:59:26.730
but there is an equivalent
in discrete time, which

00:59:26.730 --> 00:59:30.010
is essentially identical.

00:59:30.010 --> 00:59:33.370
You can derive it simply
by discretizing everything

00:59:33.370 --> 00:59:36.370
I've said, so basically, the
ordinary differential equations

00:59:36.370 --> 00:59:37.570
or the cost function.

00:59:37.570 --> 00:59:40.880
In discrete time, the
evolution of the system

00:59:40.880 --> 00:59:44.460
is no longer described in terms
of differential equations,

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:49.960
but rather a discrete
update equations.

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:55.200
K is the discrete
time stage, time step.

00:59:55.200 --> 00:59:58.720
K plus 1 is the next time step.

00:59:58.720 --> 01:00:03.320
The cost function
is exactly the same,

01:00:03.320 --> 01:00:07.000
but now the integral has
been replaced by a summation.

01:00:07.000 --> 01:00:11.240
You have control constraints,
meaning that your control

01:00:11.240 --> 01:00:15.400
variables have to belong to
some set capital U of desired

01:00:15.400 --> 01:00:18.720
or allowed control variables.

01:00:18.720 --> 01:00:24.160
And then you want to find
a set of controls, such

01:00:24.160 --> 01:00:27.200
that your cost is minimized.

01:00:27.200 --> 01:00:29.940
So the concept is
exactly the same.

01:00:29.940 --> 01:00:32.800
And the same notion of closed
loop and open loop control

01:00:32.800 --> 01:00:33.540
applies.

01:00:36.480 --> 01:00:39.390
You're probably more familiar
about this formulation when

01:00:39.390 --> 01:00:41.430
I extend it to the
stochastic setting, which

01:00:41.430 --> 01:00:44.150
is the setting of Markov
decision processes.

01:00:44.150 --> 01:00:46.630
So we look at that
formulation a little bit

01:00:46.630 --> 01:00:48.135
later on in the class.

01:00:48.135 --> 01:00:49.510
But for now, for
the start, we'll

01:00:49.510 --> 01:00:52.530
focus more on
deterministic formulation.

01:00:55.670 --> 01:00:58.430
Any questions?

01:00:58.430 --> 01:00:59.470
Yeah?

01:00:59.470 --> 01:01:03.310
On the previous slide, the
forms of optimal control,

01:01:03.310 --> 01:01:09.630
you mentioned that the
open-loop, version e,

01:01:09.630 --> 01:01:12.270
is for a particular state value.

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:14.670
What exactly does
that have to do

01:01:14.670 --> 01:01:17.030
with whether it's open-loop
or closed-loop port

01:01:17.030 --> 01:01:18.365
in the relation of the group?

01:01:18.365 --> 01:01:20.990
I mean, the closed loop is also
a function of the initial state

01:01:20.990 --> 01:01:21.950
value.

01:01:21.950 --> 01:01:24.030
But in addition to that,
it's also a function

01:01:24.030 --> 01:01:25.853
of all the future state values.

01:01:25.853 --> 01:01:27.270
So basically, it's
telling you how

01:01:27.270 --> 01:01:30.643
to act optimally from all
possible states of the world.

01:01:30.643 --> 01:01:32.310
Keep in mind that the
state of the world

01:01:32.310 --> 01:01:35.350
may not just be modeling
where you are, but also

01:01:35.350 --> 01:01:40.230
where you are with respect to
everyone in here and a control

01:01:40.230 --> 01:01:41.230
action.

01:01:41.230 --> 01:01:44.538
The open loop control
does not take into account

01:01:44.538 --> 01:01:45.830
what will happen in the future.

01:01:45.830 --> 01:01:49.190
So once you fixed your
initial condition,

01:01:49.190 --> 01:01:51.210
and you're computing
your controlled sequence,

01:01:51.210 --> 01:01:55.830
you're going to blindly execute
on it no matter what happens.

01:01:55.830 --> 01:01:58.590
Does it make sense?

01:01:58.590 --> 01:01:59.530
Which sounds crazy.

01:01:59.530 --> 01:02:04.950
But for example, if
you want to optimize

01:02:04.950 --> 01:02:10.172
the trajectory of a spacecraft
that goes to the moon,

01:02:10.172 --> 01:02:12.630
maybe it's fine, actually, to
consider an open loop control

01:02:12.630 --> 01:02:13.670
sequence.

01:02:13.670 --> 01:02:18.910
In any case, in general, you
don't really just optimize over

01:02:18.910 --> 01:02:22.710
control sequence and then
just blindly execute it.

01:02:22.710 --> 01:02:26.090
You typically executed it in
a receding horizon manner,

01:02:26.090 --> 01:02:30.790
meaning that you
execute the first 10%.

01:02:30.790 --> 01:02:33.410
And then you open your
eyes, you see what happens,

01:02:33.410 --> 01:02:36.360
and then you recompute a new
open loop control sequence.

01:02:36.360 --> 01:02:39.640
That's what will lead us to
model predictive control.

01:02:39.640 --> 01:02:41.640
So basically, you compute
your control sequence,

01:02:41.640 --> 01:02:44.960
you close your eye, you move
a little bit, you open it,

01:02:44.960 --> 01:02:47.260
you recompute a new
open control sequence.

01:02:47.260 --> 01:02:49.200
So you're doing a
closed loop control,

01:02:49.200 --> 01:02:50.900
but at every point
in time, you're

01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:52.760
only solving open
loop control problem,

01:02:52.760 --> 01:02:54.600
which is computationally
more advantageous.

01:03:00.765 --> 01:03:03.140
It's not too difficult, but
as I said, it's a bit nuanced

01:03:03.140 --> 01:03:04.828
and one can easily get confused.

01:03:04.828 --> 01:03:07.120
So throughout this class,
you'll see a lot of examples.

01:03:07.120 --> 01:03:10.360
So by the end of this class,
you will become super obvious.

01:03:10.360 --> 01:03:11.060
Yeah?

01:03:11.060 --> 01:03:15.084
Suppose the disturbance of
this model, the disturbance--

01:03:15.084 --> 01:03:17.500
The deformulation of that model.

01:03:17.500 --> 01:03:19.940
We can model it
in different ways.

01:03:19.940 --> 01:03:23.320
We can model it
indirectly, directly,

01:03:23.320 --> 01:03:25.940
deterministically, and
directly stochastically.

01:03:25.940 --> 01:03:29.220
So for example in
this formulation here,

01:03:29.220 --> 01:03:33.620
your f can also depend
on the disturbance

01:03:33.620 --> 01:03:38.970
that we can assume to be
generated randomly according

01:03:38.970 --> 01:03:42.130
to some stochastic model
or deterministically maybe

01:03:42.130 --> 01:03:43.550
in a game theoretical setting.

01:03:43.550 --> 01:03:46.750
So we assume that nature,
for whatever reason,

01:03:46.750 --> 01:03:50.310
is trying to, within some
bounds, maximally perturb you.

01:03:50.310 --> 01:03:53.890
So this is protecting
yourself from the worst case.

01:03:53.890 --> 01:03:58.490
Or in general, you can also
develop a control policy,

01:03:58.490 --> 01:04:03.490
neglecting disturbances
explicitly and just making sure

01:04:03.490 --> 01:04:08.810
that, for example, you allow
enough buffer for your state

01:04:08.810 --> 01:04:11.233
trajectory in between
state constraints,

01:04:11.233 --> 01:04:13.150
so that even if there
is a little disturbance,

01:04:13.150 --> 01:04:14.610
you don't hit anything.

01:04:14.610 --> 01:04:17.030
So there are different ways
of modeling disturbances.

01:04:17.030 --> 01:04:20.290
But yes, if you want
to do so explicitly,

01:04:20.290 --> 01:04:22.955
you add a disturbance
variable here.

01:04:22.955 --> 01:04:24.830
And then you have to
add a disturbance model.

01:04:24.830 --> 01:04:26.570
It could be deterministic.

01:04:26.570 --> 01:04:30.050
Like worst case, nature
is going to damage you

01:04:30.050 --> 01:04:31.070
as much as possible.

01:04:31.070 --> 01:04:34.240
So you solve essentially the
optimization problem itself

01:04:34.240 --> 01:04:36.480
or stochastically, it
could be just random.

01:04:36.480 --> 01:04:40.200
And we'll see some variations
thereof throughout the class.

01:04:40.200 --> 01:04:43.080
For example, we're going to see
worst case disturbances when

01:04:43.080 --> 01:04:48.240
we talk about differential
games and then Markov

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:49.140
decision processes.

01:04:49.140 --> 01:04:52.640
We're going to look at
a lot of cool problems.

01:04:52.640 --> 01:04:57.000
Like, have you heard of
the homicidal chauffeur?

01:04:57.000 --> 01:04:59.480
So that's a problem.

01:04:59.480 --> 01:05:01.580
I'll let you think about it.

01:05:01.580 --> 01:05:04.840
And then in a few
weeks, we'll solve it.

01:05:04.840 --> 01:05:06.260
So you are a car.

01:05:09.800 --> 01:05:12.560
And you want to get from
some initial condition

01:05:12.560 --> 01:05:15.840
with some final condition.

01:05:15.840 --> 01:05:19.780
Or actually, you
have a pedestrian.

01:05:19.780 --> 01:05:21.400
You're a pedestrian.

01:05:21.400 --> 01:05:22.300
So this is you.

01:05:22.300 --> 01:05:26.320
You want to get from
some initial condition

01:05:26.320 --> 01:05:30.920
to some final condition,
but you have a disturbance.

01:05:30.920 --> 01:05:33.920
The disturbance is
that there is a car

01:05:33.920 --> 01:05:37.280
and a car wants to hit you.

01:05:37.280 --> 01:05:40.040
So you may say the
situation is desperate

01:05:40.040 --> 01:05:43.600
because the car can
go faster than you,

01:05:43.600 --> 01:05:45.630
but you are taking
this class and you know

01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:46.940
how to exploit the dynamics.

01:05:46.940 --> 01:05:51.280
So what's the difference
between a car and a pedestrian?

01:05:51.280 --> 01:05:54.240
That a car can go
faster than you,

01:05:54.240 --> 01:05:56.300
but a pedestrian
is omnidirectional.

01:05:56.300 --> 01:06:00.560
So basically, you can
turn over yourself.

01:06:00.560 --> 01:06:04.020
So instead, a car
cannot move diagonally.

01:06:04.020 --> 01:06:07.640
So at best, you compute
this type of trajectories.

01:06:07.640 --> 01:06:11.560
So the problem will be, what is
the set of initial conditions

01:06:11.560 --> 01:06:14.200
that allow you to get
to the final condition

01:06:14.200 --> 01:06:17.200
without a car hitting you?

01:06:17.200 --> 01:06:18.700
That's one way of solving it.

01:06:18.700 --> 01:06:21.880
That's one famous
optimal control problem.

01:06:21.880 --> 01:06:27.640
There are also some others,
like the lady in the lake where

01:06:27.640 --> 01:06:30.790
you are-- there is a lady that
is trying to swim to the fence,

01:06:30.790 --> 01:06:34.190
but then there is a monster
that is trying to chase her.

01:06:34.190 --> 01:06:36.510
There are a lot of things
you're going to see.

01:06:36.510 --> 01:06:39.550
So that's the worst case,
but then there is also the--

01:06:39.550 --> 01:06:43.870
we'll see how to solve
all these problems.

01:06:43.870 --> 01:06:44.430
Great.

01:06:44.430 --> 01:06:46.750
So just a few minutes left.

01:06:46.750 --> 01:06:53.710
So what we're going to do in the
next couple of lectures or so

01:06:53.710 --> 01:06:56.950
is to take a step
back and recognize

01:06:56.950 --> 01:07:00.270
that optimal control,
at the end of the day,

01:07:00.270 --> 01:07:03.650
is an optimization problem,
a very difficult one,

01:07:03.650 --> 01:07:06.590
because in general, we
are solving either a very

01:07:06.590 --> 01:07:09.393
high-dimensional problem or even
infinite dimensional problem,

01:07:09.393 --> 01:07:10.810
but still an
optimization problem.

01:07:10.810 --> 01:07:12.230
So we take a step back.

01:07:12.230 --> 01:07:16.890
We look at the topic of
classical optimization,

01:07:16.890 --> 01:07:21.910
so optimization with a
reasonable number of variables.

01:07:21.910 --> 01:07:25.670
We look at what are the main
tools available there in terms

01:07:25.670 --> 01:07:27.950
of necessary
optimality conditions,

01:07:27.950 --> 01:07:31.380
computational methods
to solve an optimum.

01:07:31.380 --> 01:07:33.440
And then throughout
the rest of the class,

01:07:33.440 --> 01:07:36.300
we'll see how we can
leverage to that intuition

01:07:36.300 --> 01:07:39.020
to develop approaches
that are tailored

01:07:39.020 --> 01:07:41.900
to our more general
optimal control problem.

01:07:41.900 --> 01:07:43.268
Does that make sense?

01:07:43.268 --> 01:07:44.560
It seems a reasonable approach.

01:07:44.560 --> 01:07:46.805
Let's first look at an
easier problem first,

01:07:46.805 --> 01:07:48.180
and then see if
there is anything

01:07:48.180 --> 01:07:51.900
we can use to solve the
problem we care about.

01:07:51.900 --> 01:07:57.980
So what do I mean about a
classical optimization problem?

01:07:57.980 --> 01:08:00.280
The simplest possible--
well, it's not the simplest,

01:08:00.280 --> 01:08:02.780
but one of the simplest
possible version

01:08:02.780 --> 01:08:06.780
is referred to as unconstrained
nonlinear programming.

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:09.060
You're given a function f.

01:08:09.060 --> 01:08:14.140
You're given a vector
x that belongs to rn.

01:08:14.140 --> 01:08:19.660
So this is a vector of n
decision variables, so n numbers

01:08:19.660 --> 01:08:20.760
that you get to pick.

01:08:23.300 --> 01:08:24.399
I lost the red one.

01:08:24.399 --> 01:08:28.172
That was the only
one that was working.

01:08:28.172 --> 01:08:28.880
Did you steal it?

01:08:28.880 --> 01:08:29.479
Where is it?

01:08:32.020 --> 01:08:32.760
The red one.

01:08:35.500 --> 01:08:42.300
So x1, x2, dot,
dot, dot, dot, xn.

01:08:42.300 --> 01:08:44.760
So this is your vector
of decision variables.

01:08:44.760 --> 01:08:47.859
You have to assign a number
to each one of these terms,

01:08:47.859 --> 01:08:52.359
like 0.3 here, 0.6.

01:08:52.359 --> 01:08:56.500
And so you want to find the
assignments to those n decision

01:08:56.500 --> 01:08:59.460
variables that minimizes
the function f of x.

01:08:59.460 --> 01:09:02.500
This is referred to as
unconstrained optimization.

01:09:02.500 --> 01:09:06.399
Typically, we assume that f is
continuously differentiable.

01:09:06.399 --> 01:09:10.060
So we can take derivatives,
and sometimes, it is twice

01:09:10.060 --> 01:09:13.220
continuously differentiable.

01:09:13.220 --> 01:09:16.819
So what does it mean for
a point to be an optimum?

01:09:16.819 --> 01:09:20.979
First of all, we have two
types of optima, local minima

01:09:20.979 --> 01:09:22.740
and global minima.

01:09:22.740 --> 01:09:28.270
Local minima means that a point
x star is a local minimum,

01:09:28.270 --> 01:09:32.029
if when you move
around that point,

01:09:32.029 --> 01:09:36.890
the function f has a
higher value, larger value.

01:09:36.890 --> 01:09:40.370
A point is a global
minimum if-- a point x star

01:09:40.370 --> 01:09:45.609
is a global minimum, if
there is no other point

01:09:45.609 --> 01:09:48.390
or no other assignment
for the state variables

01:09:48.390 --> 01:09:51.810
that would achieve
a lower cost for f.

01:09:51.810 --> 01:09:57.010
More mathematically, we say that
a point or a state or assignment

01:09:57.010 --> 01:10:01.210
of variables x star
is a local minimum,

01:10:01.210 --> 01:10:06.210
if there exists a
neighborhood of x star defined

01:10:06.210 --> 01:10:09.030
by the distance between x star.

01:10:09.030 --> 01:10:12.650
So this symbol is the
symbolic norm for distance.

01:10:12.650 --> 01:10:14.670
So there is a neighborhood
around x star.

01:10:14.670 --> 01:10:22.370
So all points that are
displaced by x star

01:10:22.370 --> 01:10:28.440
no more than epsilon, within
that neighborhood, the cost

01:10:28.440 --> 01:10:30.920
of the function-- the
value of the function f

01:10:30.920 --> 01:10:34.200
is lower than f of x star.

01:10:34.200 --> 01:10:35.340
So this is x star.

01:10:39.680 --> 01:10:45.160
This is for square
norm, the set of point

01:10:45.160 --> 01:10:49.720
whose distance from x
star is less than epsilon.

01:10:49.720 --> 01:10:51.880
We are saying that
x star is a global--

01:10:51.880 --> 01:10:55.960
is a local minimum if I
pick any point from here.

01:10:55.960 --> 01:10:59.640
So let's call this
x, f of x is greater

01:10:59.640 --> 01:11:01.220
than or equal to f of x star.

01:11:04.800 --> 01:11:07.840
It may be the case that if
I pick a point here, f of x

01:11:07.840 --> 01:11:09.700
is less than f of x star.

01:11:09.700 --> 01:11:13.240
So that's why f of x star might
just be a local minimum, but not

01:11:13.240 --> 01:11:14.360
a global minimum.

01:11:14.360 --> 01:11:17.160
If it is a global
minimum, no matter

01:11:17.160 --> 01:11:23.060
which point I pick in
r1 or r2, in this case,

01:11:23.060 --> 01:11:26.750
since I'm working on a
plane, the function f of

01:11:26.750 --> 01:11:30.910
x is greater than or
equal to f of x star.

01:11:30.910 --> 01:11:34.270
So what we'll see next
time is the notion

01:11:34.270 --> 01:11:38.290
of a necessary
optimality conditions.

01:11:38.290 --> 01:11:39.790
So necessary
optimality conditions

01:11:39.790 --> 01:11:46.630
are conditions that tell you
if x star is a local minimum,

01:11:46.630 --> 01:11:49.988
then something has to happen.

01:11:49.988 --> 01:11:52.030
Then something has to
happen, in this case, means

01:11:52.030 --> 01:11:54.990
that the gradient of f
evaluated at that point

01:11:54.990 --> 01:11:56.830
should be equal to 0.

01:11:56.830 --> 01:11:59.890
So why are necessary
optimality conditions useful?

01:11:59.890 --> 01:12:02.270
Well, for a variety
of reasons, one

01:12:02.270 --> 01:12:05.030
of them being that
they can be used as

01:12:05.030 --> 01:12:07.630
filters for candidates
for optimality.

01:12:07.630 --> 01:12:10.830
You just look at
all the points that

01:12:10.830 --> 01:12:14.270
satisfy those necessary
optimality conditions.

01:12:14.270 --> 01:12:15.830
Hopefully, there
are not too many.

01:12:15.830 --> 01:12:17.090
And then you brute force them.

01:12:17.090 --> 01:12:19.110
You compute the value
for each one of them.

01:12:19.110 --> 01:12:22.590
The one that achieves the lowest
value has to be the minimum.

01:12:22.590 --> 01:12:27.590
That's one way of looking at the
necessary optimality conditions.

01:12:27.590 --> 01:12:29.110
So we'll derive those.

01:12:29.110 --> 01:12:31.550
It'll take us about 15 minutes.

01:12:31.550 --> 01:12:33.813
We'll see that actually,
for this class of problems,

01:12:33.813 --> 01:12:35.230
the necessary
optimality condition

01:12:35.230 --> 01:12:38.270
is that the gradient of
f has to be equal to 0.

01:12:38.270 --> 01:12:40.990
Later on in the class,
we'll see that this notion

01:12:40.990 --> 01:12:43.190
of necessary
optimality condition

01:12:43.190 --> 01:12:46.110
pours over to the
optimal control problem.

01:12:46.110 --> 01:12:48.610
Unfortunately, the condition
will be a bit more complicated,

01:12:48.610 --> 01:12:51.590
but the notion is
going to be the same.

01:12:51.590 --> 01:12:52.930
So we're going to do that.

01:12:52.930 --> 01:12:58.110
And then we're going to look at
still classical optimization.

01:12:58.110 --> 01:13:01.130
But we will also
account for constraints.

01:13:01.130 --> 01:13:03.590
So we want to compute
a point that is

01:13:03.590 --> 01:13:07.630
a minimum of f subject
to some function h

01:13:07.630 --> 01:13:11.070
be less than or equal
to a number, which

01:13:11.070 --> 01:13:14.550
brings us closer to the
optimal control formulation.

01:13:14.550 --> 01:13:15.170
All right.

01:13:15.170 --> 01:13:18.080
So see you on Wednesday.

